Recording 2. Kwanzaa is a cultural festival during which African Americans celebrate

166
00:17:59,460 --> 00:18:04,020
and reflect upon their rich heritage as the products of two worlds.

167
00:18:04,420 --> 00:18:08,280
It begins December 26th lasts for seven days.

168
00:18:08,760 --> 00:18:12,940
Kwanzaa was founded in 1966 by Dr.

169
00:18:13,380 --> 00:18:18,760
Karenga, a college professor and African American leader, believed that a special

170
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:24,340
holiday could help African Americans meet their goals of building strong families,

171
00:18:24,840 --> 00:18:28,720
learning about their history, and creating a sense of unity.

172
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:34,400
After conducting extensive research in which he studied the festivals of many

173
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:40,040
African groups of people, he decided that the new holiday should be a harvest or

174
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:45,340
first fruits celebration, incorporating ideas from many different harvest

175
00:18:45,340 --> 00:18:51,820
traditions. Kwanzaa is a Kiswahili word meaning the first fruits of the harvest.

176
00:18:52,500 --> 00:18:57,880
The East African language of Kiswahili was chosen as the official language of

177
00:18:57,880 --> 00:19:03,900
Kwanzaa because it is a non -tribal language spoken by a large portion of the

178
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:05,160
African population.

179
00:19:06,060 --> 00:19:08,700
Also, its pronunciation is easy.

180
00:19:09,840 --> 00:19:13,460
Kwanzaa is based on seven principles which are unity,

181
00:19:14,220 --> 00:19:15,380
self -determination,

182
00:19:16,060 --> 00:19:18,100
collective work and responsibility,

183
00:19:19,100 --> 00:19:20,480
cooperative economics,

184
00:19:21,300 --> 00:19:24,060
purpose, creativity, and faith.

185
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:27,680
One principle is highlighted each day of the holiday.

186
00:19:28,180 --> 00:19:34,260
In preparation for the celebration, a straw mat is placed on the table along with a

187
00:19:34,260 --> 00:19:39,900
candle holder with seven candles, one black, three red, and three green.

188
00:19:40,580 --> 00:19:45,440
The black candle represents the African American people, the red is for their

189
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:49,160
struggles, and the green represents their hopes for the future.

190
00:19:50,180 --> 00:19:56,960
Other items placed on the table are a variety of fruit, ears of corns, gifts, and a

191
00:19:56,960 --> 00:20:00,340
communal unity cup for pouring and sharing drinks.

192
00:20:01,240 --> 00:20:06,480
Each day of Kwanzaa, usually before the evening meal, family and friends gather

193
00:20:06,480 --> 00:20:11,160
around the table and someone lights a candle beginning with the black.

194
00:20:11,920 --> 00:20:16,200
After that, candles are lit alternately from left to right.

195
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:21,740
While the candle is being lit, a principle is recited, then each person present

196
00:20:21,740 --> 00:20:27,620
takes a turn to speak about the importance that principle has to himself or herself.

197
00:20:28,540 --> 00:20:32,580
Next, the ceremony focuses on remembering those who have died.

198
00:20:33,240 --> 00:20:38,400
A selected person pours water or juice from the unity cup into a bowl.

199
00:20:39,040 --> 00:20:45,080
That person then drinks from the cup and raises it high saying harambee, which means

200
00:20:45,080 --> 00:20:46,620
let's all pull together.

201
00:20:47,300 --> 00:20:52,460
All repeat harambee seven times and each person drinks from the cup.

202
00:20:52,880 --> 00:20:58,640
Then names of African American leaders and heroes are called out and everyone

203
00:20:58,640 --> 00:21:01,440
reflects upon the great things these people did.

204
00:21:02,040 --> 00:21:07,120
The ceremony is followed by a meal and then singing and perhaps listening to African

205
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:39,440
music. The special holiday

